/* comparison.cfab */
/*
Shows change between oldest CFAB style and "modern"/Newest as of [31/01/2026].

Oldest style lacks most QOL features around aliases, macros, console output and even inline operations.
 + Much more ASM style
 + Simpler, (almost) every line maps to 1 finished instruction
 - Much less readable at a glance
 - Comments are single line only, and are the entirety of a line.
 - No ROM usable on the file. 

Newest style as of [31/01/2026] is much higher-level, with variable names, output, and other QOL.
 + Comments can be anywhere, even mid-line
 + Variables can be named and assigned new values in a singular line
 + Inline operations are common, even reccomended for readability if applicable
 + Macros can abstract parts of CFAB for multiple-calls of such.
 + ROM can be used to store unchanging bulk data, ditto for RAM (except it can be altered at runtime)
 - One line can be any 1+ number of finalised instructions
 - Mix of low and high level styles can be confusing ("Can I do X? Is Y implemented? If I want to do Z, is it going to fab as I expect?")
 - Abstractions of some things (Like copying ROM into RAM) can be harder to understand the usage of, as it isn't as immediately clear as doing it yourself manually.
*/


//Find average (mean) of 3 values;
//////// OLDEST ////////
//Set the 3 values
SET r0 #7
SET r1 #12
SET r2 #10

//Number of values;
SET r3 #3

//Sum and divide by 3.
ADD r0 r1
ADD rOP r2
DIV rOP r3
//////// OLDEST ////////

//////// NEWEST ////////
$sum = (#7 + #12); //First 2 values
$sum = ($sum + #10); //Second 2
$result = ($sum / #3); //Divide to find average

PRINT "The average of 7, 12 and 10 is: ";
COUT $result \n; //Show the result of the operation.
//////// NEWEST ////////





//Loop 16 times;
//////// OLDEST ////////
//Set the counter to be 0, and the number of loops to 16, the amount to add by is 1.
SET r0 #0
SET r1 #16
SET r2 #1

:oldLoop
//Do something in the loop.

//Check if it should loop or not
r0 + r2
rOP ~ r0
r0 <= r1
BRN :oldLoop
//Outside of loop.
//////// OLDEST ////////

//////// NEWEST ////////
//Set the counter to be 0, and the number of loops to 16.
$iteration = #0;
$count = #16;

//Indentation is ALWAYS optional. I will use it for readability.
:newLoop;
	//Do something in the loop

	//Check if it should loop or not
	$iteration ++; //Increment iteration (add 1)
if ($iteration <= $count) then-goto :newLoop;
//Outside of loop
//////// NEWEST ////////





//Do the same thing 3 times;
//The complex thing here will be use of MOD then ADD, for example's sake.
//////// OLDEST ////////
//Must be called 3 times manually
MOD r0 r1
ADD r0 rOP

MOD r1 r2
ADD r0 rOP

MOD r2 r3
ADD r0 rOP
//If it was longer, this would not be ideal
//////// OLDEST ////////

//////// NEWEST ////////
//Create a "macro" (function-like) that contains the complex thing.
define %complexThing $A $B as
	$A % $B;
	r0 + rOP; //For example's sake it uses fixed registers same as the original.
end

//Now we call them;
%complexThing r0 r1;
%complexThing r1 r2;
%complexThing r2 r3;
//For much longer macros this becomes good QOL quickly.
//////// NEWEST ////////



